course: dictionary
lesson: 13513
title: Being fair: impartiality
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Impartiality is a word for being fair. A person who shows impartiality does not take sides and treats everyone in the same way. «The referee showed impartiality by giving the same decisions to both teams.» The referee did not favour one team, so the decisions were fair.
explain: Impartial is the describing word: «The judge was impartial, so she did not let her friend win the case.» Impartiality is the noun, the quality of being impartial. Break it into parts: «im-» means not, «partial» means favouring one side, and «-ity» turns it into a noun. So impartiality means not favouring one side.
explain: «The newsreader kept impartiality by giving both sides of the story a fair chance.» Judges, referees, reporters and teachers all need impartiality. «Mrs Patel marked every test with strict impartiality, so no pupil got extra marks for being her favourite.»
explain: To check the meaning, put «fairness» in the sentence. If «The referee showed fairness» still makes sense, you have understood the main idea. Impartiality is a more formal word, and it is often used in news, law and sport.
fact: Impartiality means being fair and not taking sides.
fact: «Impartial» is the describing word, and «impartiality» is the noun.
fact: The prefix «im-» means not, so «impartial» means not favouring one side.
fact: «Strict impartiality» means being fair with no exceptions at all.
fact: In the news, impartiality means giving both sides of a story a fair chance.
exercise: «The referee showed impartiality by giving the same decisions to both teams.» What does impartiality mean here? | Scoring the most goals | *Being fair and not taking sides | Choosing the team he likes best | Being loud and strict with the players
exercise: Which word describes a judge who does not take sides? | Lazy | *Impartial | Biased | Noisy
exercise: Why must a judge show impartiality? | So the judge can take a friend's side | So the judge can be noisy | *So every person gets a fair trial | So the court can finish early
test: «The teacher showed impartiality when she marked both essays in the same way.» What does impartiality mean? | Giving the best marks to her favourite pupil | Writing the essays for the children | *Treating everyone fairly, without favourites | Reading the essays quickly
test: Which sentence shows impartiality? | The reporter only told one side of the story. | The judge let her brother win the case. | The referee gave a penalty to the team he liked. | *The referee made the same decisions for both teams.
exam: A news reporter must keep impartiality. What does this mean? | Ignoring one side and telling the other | Reporting only what her friends say | *Giving both sides of the story a fair chance | Choosing the most exciting story and ignoring the rest
